### Why start with data profiling?

Legacy systems accumulate decades of dirty data. You cannot design a correct transform until you know the mess you are moving.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class DataProfiling {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Legacy data problems found during profiling:");
        System.out.println("  - Dates stored as VARCHAR in 5 different formats");
        System.out.println("  - Phone numbers: some with country code, some without");
        System.out.println("  - Duplicate customer records (same PAN, different IDs)");
        System.out.println("  - Balance stored as comma-separated string: '9,50,000.00'");
        System.out.println("  - Status codes: 'A', 'ACT', 'ACTIVE' all mean active");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Profile BEFORE designing the transform");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Legacy data problems found during profiling:
  - Dates as VARCHAR in 5 formats
  - Duplicate customers (same PAN, different IDs)
  - Balance as comma-separated string
  - Status codes: 'A', 'ACT', 'ACTIVE' all mean active

Profile BEFORE designing the transform
```

### How does the staging zone help?

Export to cloud storage first. If the load fails, re-run from staging without touching the fragile legacy system.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class StagingZone {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Staging in S3/GCS:");
        System.out.println("  1. Export legacy DB to CSV/Parquet -> upload to S3");
        System.out.println("  2. S3 is durable, cheap, and replayable");
        System.out.println("  3. If load fails: re-run from S3 (not legacy)");
        System.out.println("  4. Legacy system never touched again after export");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Staging in S3/GCS:
  1. Export legacy -> S3
  2. Durable, replayable
  3. Failures re-run from S3
  4. Legacy untouched after export
```

### How does the ETL transform work?

Clean, validate, map old codes, parse amounts into BigDecimal, and reject bad records.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransformStep {

    public static BigDecimal parseAmount(String legacyAmount) {
        // Legacy: "9,50,000.00" (Indian format with commas)
        String cleaned = legacyAmount.replace(",", "");
        return new BigDecimal(cleaned);
    }

    public static String mapStatus(String legacyStatus) {
        return switch (legacyStatus.toUpperCase()) {
            case "A", "ACT", "ACTIVE" -> "ACTIVE";
            case "C", "CLS", "CLOSED" -> "CLOSED";
            case "D", "DOR", "DORMANT" -> "DORMANT";
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown status: " + legacyStatus);
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Transform: " + parseAmount("9,50,000.00") + " = " + new BigDecimal("950000.00"));
        System.out.println("Map status: 'ACT' -> " + mapStatus("ACT"));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Transform: 950000.00 = 950000.00
Map status: 'ACT' -> ACTIVE
```

### Why must the load be idempotent?

Migrations get interrupted. An idempotent load via upserts keyed on legacy ID means re-running updates existing rows instead of creating duplicates.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class IdempotentLoad {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Idempotent load:");
        System.out.println("  INSERT INTO customers (legacy_id, name, balance)");
        System.out.println("  VALUES ('LEG-001', 'Rajesh Kumar', 950000.00)");
        System.out.println("  ON CONFLICT (legacy_id) DO UPDATE SET");
        System.out.println("    name = EXCLUDED.name, balance = EXCLUDED.balance;");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Run 1: INSERT Rajesh -> new row");
        System.out.println("  Run 2: CONFLICT -> UPDATE Rajesh -> same row, no duplicate");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Checkpoint progress so restart resumes from last batch");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Idempotent load:
  Run 1: INSERT Rajesh -> new row
  Run 2: CONFLICT -> UPDATE Rajesh -> same row, no duplicate
  Checkpoint progress so restart resumes from last batch
```

### How does reconciliation prove correctness?

Compare counts and balance totals between source and target. Run both systems in parallel before cutover.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Reconciliation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long legacyCount = 1_500_000;
        BigDecimal legacyTotalBalance = new BigDecimal("45000000000.00");

        long cloudCount = 1_500_000;
        BigDecimal cloudTotalBalance = new BigDecimal("45000000000.00");

        boolean countMatch = legacyCount == cloudCount;
        boolean balanceMatch = legacyTotalBalance.compareTo(cloudTotalBalance) == 0;

        System.out.println("Reconciliation:");
        System.out.println("  Customers: legacy=" + legacyCount + ", cloud=" + cloudCount
                + " -> " + (countMatch ? "MATCH" : "MISMATCH"));
        System.out.println("  Total balance: legacy=" + legacyTotalBalance + ", cloud=" + cloudTotalBalance
                + " -> " + (balanceMatch ? "MATCH" : "MISMATCH"));
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Only cut over writes when reconciliation is 100% clean");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Reconciliation:
  Customers: legacy=1500000, cloud=1500000 -> MATCH
  Total balance: legacy=45000000000.00, cloud=45000000000.00 -> MATCH

  Only cut over writes when reconciliation is 100% clean
```

### How do you handle security for banking data?

TLS in transit, KMS at rest, least-privilege IAM, PII masking in non-production.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class MigrationSecurity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Security for banking master data:");
        System.out.println("  In transit: TLS between legacy and S3, S3 and cloud DB");
        System.out.println("  At rest: KMS encryption on S3 buckets and database");
        System.out.println("  IAM: least-privilege roles for migration service");
        System.out.println("  PII: mask Aadhaar, PAN, phone in non-production");
        System.out.println("  Audit: log every access to staging and production data");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Security for banking master data:
  In transit: TLS
  At rest: KMS encryption
  IAM: least-privilege
  PII: masked in non-production
  Audit: every access logged
```

### How do you expose the migrated data?

Clean versioned REST APIs with an anti-corruption facade during transition.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class ApiDesign {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("API design:");
        System.out.println("  GET /api/v1/customers/{id}");
        System.out.println("  GET /api/v1/accounts/{id}");
        System.out.println("  Versioned from day one");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  During transition: anti-corruption facade");
        System.out.println("    Old consumers call familiar endpoints");
        System.out.println("    Facade routes to new cloud store");
        System.out.println("    Old consumers migrate at their own pace");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
API design:
  GET /api/v1/customers/{id}
  GET /api/v1/accounts/{id}
  Versioned from day one
  Anti-corruption facade during transition
```

### Why must the migration be in CI/CD?

The migration code, schema, and infrastructure are tested and automated, not a hand-run script.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class MigrationCiCd {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("CI/CD for migration:");
        System.out.println("  1. Unit tests on transform logic (parse amounts, map statuses)");
        System.out.println("  2. Schema migrations via Flyway (versioned, reproducible)");
        System.out.println("  3. Dry run against sample data in CI");
        System.out.println("  4. Deploy migration tooling as a tested artifact");
        System.out.println("  5. NOT a hand-run script on someone's laptop");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
CI/CD for migration:
  1. Unit tests on transform logic
  2. Schema migrations via Flyway
  3. Dry run in CI
  4. Deploy as tested artifact
  5. NOT a hand-run script
```

### What about monitoring and rejected records?

Track per-record progress. Alert on rejection spikes. Capture rejects with reasons.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class MigrationMonitoring {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Migration observability:");
        System.out.println("  Metrics: extracted=1500000, transformed=1499950, loaded=1499950, rejected=50");
        System.out.println("  Rejected records table: legacy_id, reason, raw_data");
        System.out.println("  Alert: rejection rate > 0.1% -> investigate");
        System.out.println("  Dashboard: progress bar, records/sec, ETA");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Migration observability:
  extracted=1500000, transformed=1499950, loaded=1499950, rejected=50
  Rejected records: legacy_id, reason, raw_data
  Alert: rejection rate > 0.1%
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Always keep a rollback plan. Profile before designing. Reconcile before cutting over. Automate everything.

```text
Senior migration checklist:
  [x] Profile legacy data before designing transform
  [x] Stage in S3/GCS (durable, replayable)
  [x] ETL: clean, validate, map, reject bad records
  [x] Idempotent load: upserts + checkpoints
  [x] Reconciliation: counts + balance totals must match
  [x] Parallel run: serve from new, validate against old
  [x] Security: TLS, KMS, IAM, PII masking, audit
  [x] CI/CD: migration code is tested and automated
  [x] Rollback plan: always available
```
